{"version_id": 53, "article_id": 6, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Science Needs Fairer Rules When Countries Disagree", "title_zh": "国家之间有矛盾时，科学合作需要更公平的规则", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 183, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "Science has often brought countries together, even when politics pulls them apart. However, two researchers warn that this is changing, because a scientist's university is now seen as a political label.", "lead_zh": "科学常常能把不同国家拉到一起，哪怕政治上彼此对立。不过，两位研究者提醒，这种情况正在改变，因为科学家所在的大学如今被当成了政治标签。", "preview": "Scientific collaboration has long been seen as one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Although countries may disagree, resear", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科学合作被视为少数能超越地缘政治的领域之一。尽管国家之间可能意见不合，研究人员通常仍会继续合作。然而，全球紧张局势正在以新的方式影响国际研究，因此这一传统正承受压力。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 43, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/6/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 77.45, "size_bytes": 468432, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 208, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/53.json", "paragraphs": ["Scientific collaboration has long been seen as one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Although countries may disagree, researchers have usually kept working together. However, global tensions are now shaping international research in new ways, so this tradition is under pressure.", "The two authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi, work at Shanghai University and Zhejiang Normal University in China. They wrote a letter in the journal Nature on 6 October 2026. In their view, an unintended problem is appearing: where a scientist works is becoming a proxy for political identity.", "This means researchers may be judged by their institution rather than by their work. Because of this, some partnerships could break down, although the science itself has nothing to do with politics. If this trend continues, important projects may slow down or stop.", "The authors argue that fairer rules are needed to protect global science collaboration during conflict. They do not say exactly what those rules should be, but they clearly want institutions to treat researchers as individuals. Otherwise, they warn, geopolitics will keep pushing science apart."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科学合作被视为少数能超越地缘政治的领域之一。尽管国家之间可能意见不合，研究人员通常仍会继续合作。然而，全球紧张局势正在以新的方式影响国际研究，因此这一传统正承受压力。", "两位作者埃马纽埃尔·奇迪埃贝雷·埃德和埃希祖埃伦·迈克尔·米切尔·奥莫鲁伊，分别在中国上海大学和浙江师范大学工作。他们于2026年10月6日在《自然》杂志上发表了一封来信。在他们看来，一个意想不到的问题正在出现：科学家的工作单位正在变成政治身份的替身。", "这意味着研究人员可能因为所在机构而不是研究成果受到评判。因此，一些合作关系可能会破裂，尽管科学本身与政治毫无关系。如果这一趋势持续下去，重要项目可能会放缓甚至停止。", "作者认为，在冲突期间，需要更公平的规则来保护全球科学合作。他们没有明确说明这些规则应该是什么，但显然希望机构把研究人员当作个人来看待。他们警告说，否则地缘政治会继续把科学拆散。"], "body": "Scientific collaboration has long been seen as one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Although countries may disagree, researchers have usually kept working together. However, global tensions are now shaping international research in new ways, so this tradition is under pressure.\n\nThe two authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi, work at Shanghai University and Zhejiang Normal University in China. They wrote a letter in the journal Nature on 6 October 2026. In their view, an unintended problem is appearing: where a scientist works is becoming a proxy for political identity.\n\nThis means researchers may be judged by their institution rather than by their work. Because of this, some partnerships could break down, although the science itself has nothing to do with politics. If this trend continues, important projects may slow down or stop.\n\nThe authors argue that fairer rules are needed to protect global science collaboration during conflict. They do not say exactly what those rules should be, but they clearly want institutions to treat researchers as individuals. Otherwise, they warn, geopolitics will keep pushing science apart.", "body_zh": "长期以来，科学合作被视为少数能超越地缘政治的领域之一。尽管国家之间可能意见不合，研究人员通常仍会继续合作。然而，全球紧张局势正在以新的方式影响国际研究，因此这一传统正承受压力。\n\n两位作者埃马纽埃尔·奇迪埃贝雷·埃德和埃希祖埃伦·迈克尔·米切尔·奥莫鲁伊，分别在中国上海大学和浙江师范大学工作。他们于2026年10月6日在《自然》杂志上发表了一封来信。在他们看来，一个意想不到的问题正在出现：科学家的工作单位正在变成政治身份的替身。\n\n这意味着研究人员可能因为所在机构而不是研究成果受到评判。因此，一些合作关系可能会破裂，尽管科学本身与政治毫无关系。如果这一趋势持续下去，重要项目可能会放缓甚至停止。\n\n作者认为，在冲突期间，需要更公平的规则来保护全球科学合作。他们没有明确说明这些规则应该是什么，但显然希望机构把研究人员当作个人来看待。他们警告说，否则地缘政治会继续把科学拆散。", "text": "Scientific collaboration has long been seen as one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Although countries may disagree, researchers have usually kept working together. However, global tensions are now shaping international research in new ways, so this tradition is under pressure. The two authors, Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi, work at Shanghai University and Zhejiang Normal University in China. They wrote a letter in the journal Nature on 6 October 2026. In their view, an unintended problem is appearing: where a scientist works is becoming a proxy for political identity. This means researchers may be judged by their institution rather than by their work. Because of this, some partnerships could break down, although the science itself has nothing to do with politics. If this trend continues, important projects may slow down or stop. The authors argue that fairer rules are needed to protect global science collaboration during conflict. They do not say exactly what those rules should be, but they clearly want institutions to treat researchers as individuals. Otherwise, they warn, geopolitics will keep pushing science apart.", "vocab": [{"word": "collaboration", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作", "note": "常与 between/with 连用"}, {"word": "geopolitics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "地缘政治", "note": "指国家间政治与地理的关系"}, {"word": "tension", "pos": "noun", "zh": "紧张局势", "note": "常用复数 tensions"}, {"word": "institution", "pos": "noun", "zh": "机构；大学", "note": "这里指科研单位"}, {"word": "affiliation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隶属关系；工作单位", "note": "常指所属机构"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "替身；代表", "note": "a proxy for 表示“某物的替代”"}, {"word": "unintended", "pos": "adj", "zh": "意想不到的；非故意的", "note": "反义词 intended"}, {"word": "partnership", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作关系", "note": "可指机构间合作"}, {"word": "trend", "pos": "noun", "zh": "趋势", "note": "常用短语 a growing trend"}, {"word": "protect", "pos": "verb", "zh": "保护", "note": "protect ... from/against"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-va